    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>SystÃ¨me de cote ÃnerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="1" build="19">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.1b19</English>
                <French>v15.1b19</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="11">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.11</English>
                <French>v1.11</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>Locations that I found air leaks include: around most of the window and door  frames, the furnace chimney damper and fresh air supply, where the main electrical conduit penetrates the wall above the electrical panel, dryer vent, vacuum exhaust penetration through outside wall, electrical outlets on exterior walls, ceiling light and light switches in the hall and bedrooms, hall smoke alarm, attic hatch, bathroom fans and the bathroom skylight. 

 The leaks through the electrical outlets, light switches, ceiling lights, and smoke alarm can be reduced by installing foam gaskets behind the faceplates, or foaming in behind the boxes to seal the holes where the wires penetrate the plastic boots. You can foam around the wall penetrations for the electrical conduit and vacuum exhaust. The bathroom fans and dryer are probaby leaking at the exterior hoods (sometimes they stick open). Broan makes a better hood with a styrofoam ball that sealls the opening.

 The furnace fresh air supply you have is basically an open tube that is constantly dumping cold air into the furnace room. That could be replaced with an electronically controlled damper that opens when the burner comes on, to supply fresh air, then closes when the burner goes off.  </airSeal>
                <windows>Being just double glazed, the windows are the greatest source of heat loss in your house. There are not a lot of windows in the house so this seems to be a disproportionate amount of heat loss. As upgrades I replaced all the windows, including in the doors, with triple glazed, LowE, argon filled units. This reduced the heat loss through the windows by about 53 %. Replacing the windows with quadruple glazed units would see even greater savings. There was air leaking around all the window and door frames as well. It wasn t a huge amount and could be reduced by adding more sealing foam around them or, if you decide to replace them, being careful about sealing the new windows.</windows>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
